        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Machines</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these machines. The picks were made from manufacturer specifications
            and manuals, published hands-on reviews, retailer listings, warranty terms and the US
            Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database, all checked on 16 October 2026.
            The BowFlex T6 has
            <a href="/bowflex-t6-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">its own review on this site</a>,
            which is also a reading of the published evidence rather than a hands-on test.
          </p>
          <p>
            Four rules decided the list. <strong>Currently sold:</strong> each machine had to be on
            sale on its maker's own store, under that name, on the day we checked.
            <strong>Agreement:</strong> capacity, speed or resistance range, and size had to match
            between the maker and a source that does not sell the machine. <strong>Support:</strong>
            every brand here sells direct and publishes its warranty terms. <strong>No safety
            action:</strong> we searched the CPSC's recall database by brand for every maker on the
            list and checked its published warnings.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What we left out, and why</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>BowFlex Max Trainer M5 and M7.</strong> We have reviewed both, but on 16 October
            2026 BowFlex's store listed only the Max Trainer M6 and the Max Total 16. A machine its
            maker no longer sells new is not a pick; if you find an M7 secondhand, our
            <a href="/bowflex-max-trainer-m7/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Max Trainer M7 review</a>
            still applies.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>NordicTrack RW900 rower.</strong> In October 2025 iFIT recalled NordicTrack
            rowing machines with model number NTRW19147 because the console can overheat and ignite.
            <strong>Original Peloton Bike+.</strong> In November 2025 Peloton recalled Original
            Series Bike+ units with model number PL02 because the seat post can break. Both recalls
            offer repairs, but neither machine is one we would point a new buyer towards, and both
            are worth checking carefully if you are offered one secondhand.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sperax walking pads.</strong> The CPSC has warned consumers to stop using Sperax
            walking pads and treadmills immediately, and the seller has not agreed to a recall. They
            are excluded from every list on this site.
          </p>
          <p>
            We also passed over machines whose figures could not be settled on the day. The most
            common cause was a model refresh: the maker's page describes the new version and the
            reviews describe the old one. Two picks here, the Sole E35 and the walking pad, carry
            notes for exactly that reason.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="machine-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What Each Cardio Machine Is Best At</h2>
<p>
            The full decision — by goal, by joints, by space and by noise — is in our
            <a href="/home-cardio-equipment-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home cardio equipment guide</a>.
            This is the one-line version for each machine on this page, with the head-to-head
            comparisons if you are torn between two.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Treadmill:</strong> the most natural movement and the easiest to start,
            and the only machine that trains running itself. Largest footprint, most noise and
            impact. Torn? Read
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-elliptical/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs elliptical</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Exercise bike:</strong> the quietest and most compact, with no impact; it
            trains legs and fitness, not the upper body. See
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-exercise-bike/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs exercise bike</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Rowing machine:</strong> legs, back and arms together, seated and low
            impact, but it rewards technique. See
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-rowing-machine/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs rowing machine</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Elliptical:</strong> a gliding, low-impact stride with arm handles, good
            for long steady sessions. Tall and heavy.</li>
            <li><strong>Air bike:</strong> arms and legs against a fan whose resistance rises the
            harder you push, built for short, hard intervals. Loud, and brutally honest.</li>
            <li><strong>Stepper:</strong> a climbing motion that is hard on the legs and lungs and
            easy on the joints, in a small footprint. See
            <a href="/stairmaster-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">StairMaster vs treadmill</a>.</li>
            <li><strong>Walking pad:</strong> walking only, under a desk or in front of the
            television, and stored flat under a bed or sofa.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If two of these appeal equally, choose the one you would be happy to use on a tired
            evening. Consistency matters more than which machine is theoretically better, and no
            specification predicts it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gym-machines" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Cardio Machines at the Gym, and Which Translate to Home</h2>
<p>
            Most people's idea of a cardio machine comes from a gym floor, and some of those
            machines move into a house far better than others. Here is how the familiar gym cardio
            machines translate. Our guide to
            <a href="/commercial-gym-equipment/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial gym equipment</a>
            covers buying the real commercial versions in more depth.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Translates almost exactly</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Rowers.</strong> The Concept2 on this page is the same model you will find in many
            gyms and rowing clubs. It weighs 57 lb, splits in two and needs no outlet, so a home
            buyer gets the gym experience without compromise. <strong>Air bikes.</strong> The air
            bikes in functional-fitness gyms are the same class of machine as the AirDyne AD7: no
            motor, no outlet, a fan for resistance. The home versions are effectively the gym
            versions. <strong>Indoor cycling bikes.</strong> A home bike like the IC4 is lighter than
            a club studio bike, but the riding position and magnetic resistance are close enough that
            classes translate directly.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Translates with compromises</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Treadmills.</strong> A gym treadmill is heavier, longer, usually non-folding and
            built for many hours a day, and some commercial models need a dedicated electrical
            circuit. A home folding treadmill like the T6 gives you the same deck size and incline
            but a lighter frame and a shorter duty cycle. If you want the gym feel specifically, our
            <a href="/gym-treadmill-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym treadmill for home</a>
            guide explains what buying a commercial or ex-gym treadmill involves.
            <strong>Ellipticals.</strong> Gym ellipticals are longer-stride and heavier; good home
            models like the E35 come close on stride and flywheel but not on bulk, and most will not
            fit through a narrow doorway assembled.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Rarely translates</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Revolving-stair climbers.</strong> The machines with a moving staircase are
            tall, very heavy and expensive, and few homes have the ceiling height or floor for one.
            The home answer is a stepper-type machine such as the Max Trainer M6, which gives a
            similar climbing effort in a fraction of the space; our
            <a href="/stairmaster-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">StairMaster vs treadmill</a>
            comparison explains the difference in effort. <strong>Arc trainers and other
            specialist gym machines</strong> rarely come in home versions at all; a good elliptical
            or stepper is the closest substitute.
          </p>
          <p>
            One more translation worth making: a gym gives you every machine. At home you get one or
            two, so the choice matters more, and it is worth trying the movement at a gym before you
            buy the machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="specs-that-matter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. The Specifications That Matter on Each Machine</h2>
<p>
            Every machine type has two or three figures that decide whether it will suit you, and
            several that are marketing. These are the ones worth comparing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Treadmills: deck size, incline and maximum user weight.</strong> A 20 by 60-inch
            deck is the comfortable size for running; walkers can manage with less. Motor horsepower
            matters, but makers rate it differently — BowFlex footnotes its figure as a test
            rating that does not reflect operating output — so compare it only within a brand. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains why.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Bikes: resistance type, adjustability and maximum user weight.</strong>
            Magnetic resistance is quiet and needs no maintenance; felt-pad friction resistance is
            cheaper and wears. Check that the seat and handlebars adjust in both directions to fit
            your height. The number of resistance levels is less important than how finely they are
            spaced.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Rowers: resistance type, length and storage.</strong> Air, magnetic and water
            rowers feel different and make different noises. Check the length in use and how the
            rower stores — standing on end or splitting in two — before the brand.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Ellipticals: stride length, flywheel weight and step-up height.</strong> A
            20-inch stride suits most adults; shorter strides feel choppy for taller users. A heavier
            flywheel generally gives a smoother motion. Step-up height plus your own height decides
            whether you will hit the ceiling.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Air bikes: drive type and console.</strong> Belt drives are quieter and need
            less maintenance than chains. Resistance is set by your effort, so "levels" do not
            apply.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Steppers: maximum user weight, ceiling height and warranty.</strong> A stepper
            lifts you as you climb; makers give a minimum ceiling height, and it is worth measuring.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walking pads: belt length, speed range and real capacity.</strong> Belt length
            decides comfort, a 4 mph ceiling means walking only, and capacity figures differ between
            listings more than any other number. Our
            <a href="/walking-pad-buying-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad buying guide</a>
            covers the details.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-spend" class="scroll-mt-24">
