              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stepper</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$999 (Max Trainer M6)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Max Total 16 at $1,999</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Elliptical</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,699.99 (E35)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sole E25 at $1,199.99; Schwinn 411 at $699</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Three things stand out. First, a good walking pad costs a third of a good bike, but it
            only does one job. Second, the five mid-priced types — bike, treadmill, rower, air bike
            and stepper — all cluster between about $800 and $1,000 on sale, so price should not
            decide between them; the movement should. Third, ellipticals are the most expensive type
            to buy well, because a smooth stride needs a heavy flywheel and a long, rigid frame.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sale prices on most of these machines were $200 to $400 below list when we checked. If a
            machine is at full price, it is usually worth waiting; our guide to the
            <a href="/best-time-of-year-to-buy-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best time of year to buy a treadmill</a>
            explains the sale calendar, which applies to most cardio equipment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="apps-subscriptions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Apps, Screens and Subscriptions</h2>
<p>
            Most cardio machines now connect to an app, and the business model behind that app
            often matters more than the machine's screen. Every machine on this page works without a
            subscription. That was deliberate.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Bring-your-own-screen machines</strong> — the T6, IC4 and Max Trainer M6 —
            have a basic console and a holder for your phone or tablet. They connect over Bluetooth
            to apps you choose: BowFlex lists JRNY, Peloton and Zwift for the T6, and Schwinn lists
            JRNY, Peloton, Zwift and others for the IC4. Each of those apps needs its own
            subscription; check the current price, as these change often. The advantage is that if
            an app raises its price or shuts down, the machine carries on.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Built-in-screen machines</strong> like the Sole E35, with its 10.1-inch
            touchscreen, put the content on the machine. That is more convenient, but the screen
            ages faster than the frame, and the content is tied to whatever the maker supports.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Machines with no app at all</strong> — the AirDyne AD7 runs on batteries and
            needs no outlet — are the simplest to own. The Concept2's monitor does connect to apps,
            but it works perfectly well alone.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are weighing up a subscription-first machine, our guide to the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills without subscriptions</a>
            sets out what you give up and what you save.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setting-up" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Delivery, Power, Flooring and Noise</h2>
<p>
            The practical side of owning a cardio machine is where most disappointments start, and
            it differs more between machine types than people expect.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Delivery and assembly.</strong> A treadmill or elliptical arrives in a box well
            over 200 lb, often on a pallet left at the kerb unless you pay for room-of-choice
            delivery. Plan the route through the house, measure doorways and stairs, and consider
            paid assembly for the two heaviest machine types. A bike, rower, air bike or walking pad
            can usually be carried in and assembled by one or two people.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Power.</strong> Treadmills, walking pads and most ellipticals plug in; the
            rower and air bike on this page do not need to. Plug a treadmill directly into a wall
            outlet rather than an extension lead, and check the manual for the circuit it expects.
            Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill extension cords</a>
            explains why.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Flooring.</strong> A mat protects the floor from scuffs, sweat and the grit
            that wears a belt, and steadies a machine on an uneven surface. It reduces some
            vibration but does not soundproof a room. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill mats</a>
            roundup applies to every machine here.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Noise.</strong> Roughly, from quietest to loudest: magnetic bike, walking pad
            at walking pace, elliptical, stepper, treadmill at running pace, air rower, air bike.
            Fans are loud by design, and a running treadmill adds footfall that travels through
            floors. For flats and upstairs rooms, the bike or elliptical is the safer choice.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Ceiling height.</strong> Treadmills with incline, ellipticals and steppers all
            lift you. BowFlex, for example, gives the T6's minimum ceiling height as your height
            plus 20.75 inches. Measure before you order, not after.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reading-listings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Reading a Cardio Machine Listing</h2>
<p>
            Three things caught us out while checking the machines on this page, and they will catch
            out anyone comparing listings.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Model refreshes under the same name.</strong> Sole updated the E35 for 2026
            with a new console. Older coverage describes a smaller display and a different
            capacity, and we found conflicting figures for its length and unit weight. If two
            sources disagree on a machine with the same name, assume one of them is describing a
            different version, and trust the maker's current spec sheet and manual.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Motor claims on walking pads.</strong> One deal listing for the UREVO SpaceWalk 3S
            calls its motor brushless; an independent tracker that reads the manual says the manual
            and current product page do not, and treats it as brushed. Peak horsepower on a pad
            tells you very little either way. We left the motor type off the card.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Discontinued machines still for sale.</strong> Marketplace listings can carry
            on long after a maker stops selling a model new, as the BowFlex Max Trainer M5 and M7
            show. That is not necessarily bad — it may be a bargain — but check that parts and
            warranty support still exist before you buy.
          </p>
          <p>
            And the usual rules apply: treat "was" prices with suspicion, look for the warranty in
            writing, and check the seller. Our guide to
            <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill on Amazon</a>
            covers the general method, and it works for every machine type here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            A cardio machine is the right purchase for most people who want to exercise at home.
            Several buyers should think again before spending.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you already pay for a gym you use,</strong> a home machine may duplicate it.
            Our
            <a href="/home-treadmill-vs-gym-membership/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home treadmill vs gym membership</a>
            comparison sets out when owning pays off.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want to run seriously,</strong> the treadmill is the only machine here
            that trains it, and you may want a heavier non-folding model; see the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for home</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you prefer gliding cardio,</strong> the
            <a href="/best-elliptical-machines-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best elliptical machines for home</a>
            roundup has cheaper and more compact options than the E35.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you live upstairs or share walls,</strong> skip the treadmill and air bike
            and look at the bike or elliptical. A walking pad at walking pace is a reasonable
            compromise.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you have a heart, joint or balance condition,</strong> talk to your doctor
            before choosing, and favour machines with full handrails or a seat. We review
            equipment; this is not medical advice.
          </p>
          <p>
            And if you are still unsure what kind of machine you want, read the
            <a href="/home-cardio-equipment-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">decision guide</a>
            before any product page. It will save you more money than any sale.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best cardio machine for home use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The one you will use most. For most people that is a treadmill, because walking is the easiest habit to keep. If you want quiet and low impact, a magnetic exercise bike; for full-body work in little floor space, a rowing machine. Our picks are the BowFlex T6, Schwinn IC4 and Concept2 RowErg, but choose the type before the brand.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which cardio machine burns the most calories?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The one you can work hardest on for longest. Machines that use arms and legs together, such as air bikes, rowers and ellipticals, let you do more total work at a given effort, but the figure on any console is an estimate. Choose a machine you enjoy enough to use often; that decides results far more than the machine type.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which cardio machine is easiest on the knees?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Seated machines and those where your feet never leave the pedals put the least impact through the joints: exercise bikes, rowing machines and ellipticals. A stepper is low impact but works the knees through a deep range. If you have a knee condition, ask a physiotherapist which movement suits you before buying; we review equipment, not injuries.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What cardio machines are at the gym?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most gyms have treadmills, upright and recumbent bikes, ellipticals, rowing machines, stair climbers, and often air bikes and indoor cycling bikes. Rowers, air bikes and cycling bikes translate almost exactly to home versions. Treadmills and ellipticals translate with compromises in weight and duty cycle. Revolving-stair climbers rarely fit in a house.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a treadmill or an exercise bike better for home?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A treadmill suits walkers and runners and trains a movement you use every day. A bike is quieter, smaller, cheaper for the quality, and easier on the joints, which makes it the better choice for flats and upstairs rooms. If you are unsure, our treadmill vs exercise bike comparison goes through the trade-offs in detail.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do home cardio machines need a subscription?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                None of the machines on this page do. Several connect to apps such as JRNY, Peloton or Zwift, and those apps charge their own subscriptions, so check the current price. Machines with a built-in screen often tie the best content to the maker's own service. A machine that works fully from its own console is cheaper to own over time.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Cardio Machine for Home</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most cardio machine regrets come from the room, the floor or the habit, not from the machine itself.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing by specification instead of by movement</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A machine with better figures is no use if you dislike the motion. Try the movement first — at a gym, a friend’s house or a shop — and buy the type you would choose on a tired evening. Then compare specifications within that type. The decision guide linked on this page is built around exactly that order.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring ceiling height</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Treadmills on incline, ellipticals and steppers all lift you well above the floor. Makers publish minimum ceiling heights, often as your height plus a set figure, and a machine that fits the floor can still put your head in the ceiling. Measure the room height where the machine will stand before you order.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a loud machine in the wrong room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">An air bike, an air rower or a treadmill at running pace will be heard elsewhere in the house and, upstairs, by neighbours. A mat helps with vibration but does not soundproof a room. If noise matters, choose a magnetic bike or elliptical, or put the loud machine in a garage or ground-floor room.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting an old review of a refreshed machine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Makers update machines under the same name, and reviews from before the update can describe a different console, capacity or size. If a figure in a review does not match the maker’s current page, assume the review is describing the earlier version. Trust the current spec sheet and manual for the unit you are buying.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

